ging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. Might be 1.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented. 4 easy low cost non-radical changes. 3 some changes reasonably feasible. 2 significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible. 1 totally infeasible.\n\nWrapper library is reasonably feasible, some changes. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Implementing mandatory wrapper around core inference APIs piping to immutable store is non-trivial but doable. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable. 4 common strategies violate no norms. This is compliant, acceptable. So 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related. Stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This does X to get Y where Y is part of stated problem: automatic recording of events. Yes direct. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem. 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Will guarantee technical availability of required records. It captures timestamps, input metadata, system states. Article 12 requires logs over lifetime, recording events relevant... For high-risk systems in point 1(a) Annex III need specific items: period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Wrapper captures timestamps, input metadata, system states but may not capture reference database etc nor identification of persons. So partial? Might be reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Could be 4? It guarantees technical availability of required records generally. But specific requirements for certain systems may not be fully met. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says automatically captures event timestamps, input metadata, system states during model execution. Piping logs to immutable centralized store. Eliminates manual logging effort while guaranteeing technical availability of required records.\n\nIt may not capture all specifics like identification of natural persons involved in verification. But overall solves logging mandate. I'd say effectiveness 3? Or 4? \"will solve the stated problem without regard for workability\" - does it solve? Probably contributes but not full guarantee for all Article 12(3) details. Might be partial.\n\nI'll give effectiveness 3.\n\nNow produce output echoing id P1516. For each subscale: <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nFormat likely lines.\n\nProvide:\n\nP1516\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The wrapper-based telemetry library is a common observability pattern already widely used for compliance logging. => score 1\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal treats compliance as standard logging practice with a new tool but leaves the lab’s R&D workflow and research agenda unchanged. => score 1 (or 2). Let's choose 1.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a standardized library and wrapping core inference APIs requires engineering effort but is a reasonably feasible software change. => 3\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automatic immutable logging is legally required and socially uncontroversial in regulated AI development. =>4\n\nApplicability evidence: The wrapper directly addresses the mandate to technically allow automatic recording of events over the system lifetime. =>4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It will provide generic logs and reduce manual effort, but does not explicitly ensure the specific Article 12(3) fields such as reference database or verifier identification. =>3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
