ty (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. The problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Creating standard addresses logging mandate generally, but does it apply to lab's own compliance? It may be more about leadership than meeting own logs. Could be solves implied problem related: positioning lab as leader. Hmm.\n\nApplicability anchored: 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Creating standard does provide a way to meet Article 12? Possibly indirectly. Might be 3 = solves an implied problem that is related (do X to get implied Y which applies). Implied problem is to influence regulation / reduce compliance cost via standardization. I'd say applicability = 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nOpen-source standard positions lab as leader in regulatory-technical standardization. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? Possibly changes relationship: from passive compliance to active shaping. Could be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship, new partners. Not necessarily breaking paradigm (doesn't address larger problem of what lab researches). Might be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, i