ork, even if you could do it)\" That seems harsh.\n\nI'll choose applicability 3? Hmm.\n\nLet's parse instrument: relevance dimensions each through two subscales. Applicability is degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Effectiveness degree to which idea will solve problem.\n\nProposal addresses technical documentation auto-population, not logging. But Recital 71 mentions both logs and technical documentation as essential for traceability. Article 12 is about record-keeping logs. The proposal is mismatched.\n\nMaybe applicability =2: may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates to stated problem (do X which somehow relates). Documentation pipeline somehow relates to compliance burden.\n\nEffectiveness =2: unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work) for logging mandate.\n\nNow Novelty:\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nAutomated documentation pipeline using MLflow/W&B integration with template engine. That's a tool/process introduction, still treats compliance as documentation practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools, formats) but still treats compliance as logging practice? Actually documentation practice.\n\nProbably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only