asonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem). If you could build wrapper, it would generate logs. Yes. So 4.\n\nEvidence: If implemented, automatic log generation via framework wrapper would satisfy Article 12's logging and traceability requirements for the lab's high-risk systems.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nOpen-source compliance library is common strategies that violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Publishing open-source tools for regulatory compliance is socially valued and does not conflict with legal or business norms.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nBuilding modular software library wrapper for common deep learning frameworks... That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Not easy low cost? Building a library is significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might be 2 = significant change or expensive or difficult. Hmm.\n\nLab has scarce implementation resources. Building wrapper for common frameworks is non-trivial engineering effort. Probably significant change/expensive. I'd say 2? Let's see anchor: 4 easy low cost or non-radical changes; 3 some changes or reasonably feasible measures; 2 significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Building a library from scratch is significant change and expensive. So 2.\n\nEvidence: Developing and maintaining a framework-agnostic wrapper that reliably captures Article 12 events across diverse R&D workflows requires substantial engineering.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nAnchored: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving.\n\nProposal introduces new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice? It also says increases broader scientific impact by establishing gold standard for transparent reproducible R&D. That suggests changes relationship? Maybe logging woven into science. Might be 3? The lab builds open source library for community, not just internal logging. That's changing relationship to compliance obligations (turns compliance into community offering). Could be paradigm stretching.\n\nI think slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. However it also says establishing gold standard for transparent reproducible R&D – that might stretch.\n\nLet's choose 2? Hmm. The description: \"introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". That fits open-source library. It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally? It does create new offering.\n\nMaybe paradigm_relatedness = 3: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). The proposal explicitly aims to increase broader scientific impact by establishing gold standard. That's new offering born from compliance. So 3.\n\nEvidence: The lab repurposes compliance work into an open-source product and community standard, weaving logging into its scientific identity.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious.\nOpen-source compliance library for AI Act logging. Is that common? Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination. Not expressed before? Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not necessarily ingenious. Maybe 2 = interesting.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Typical responses: hire compliance officer, buy logging tool, add manual logs. Building open-source library is unusual. So originality maybe 3.\n\nEvidence: Few labs propose releasing a reusable open-source framework wrapper specifically to meet Article 12 rather than internal tooling.\n\nThus scores:\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 4\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 2\nparadigm_relatedness 3\noriginality 3\n\nReturn format. Need \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give ... Provide in order.\n\nI'll output lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1247\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft succinct sentences.\n\n",
