        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P1127.\n\nProposal: Unified Compliance Logging Framework. standardized internal software library automatically generates logs compliant with Article 12 across all high-risk projects. provides common interface for recording timestamps and input data. software engineers build middleware API integrated into mandatory CI/CD pipeline. Every system deployment must call standardized functions to record events by default. Ensures uniform regulatory compliance while minimizing manual effort required from research scientists.\n\nRate subscales:\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is internal library middleware integrated into CI/CD. That's introducing new tool/process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally? It automates logging. Likely slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools, formats, processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So level 2.\n\nEvidence sentence: It adds a standardized middleware library for logging but keeps compliance as a separate logging requirement rather than redefining R&D.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified compliance logging framework is fairly common. Many labs build internal logging libraries. Probably common, mundane. Level 1.\n\nEvidence: Building an internal standardized logging library for regulatory compliance is a common engineering practice.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nBuilding middleware API integrated into CI/CD pipeline requires software engineers build library and enforce mandatory calls. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Might be moderate. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Developing a shared library and CI/CD enforcement is feasible with engineering effort but requires coordination across projects.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal complies with regulation. Acceptable. Level 4.\n\nEvidence: Standardizing logging to meet Article 12 aligns with legal requirements and does not offend norms.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal directly addresses meeting logging mandate via automatic logs. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The framework directly provides automatic event recording required by Article 12 for high-risk systems.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill it solve? It ensures uniform compliance while minimizing manual effort. Might not cover all logging details like reference database, natural persons etc but provides timestamps and input data. Partially effective? The proposal says automatically generates logs compliant with Article 12 across all high-risk projects, provides common interface for recording timestamps and input data. Article 12 requires specific events, reference database, identification of natural persons etc. It may be partial. But rating says if you could do it, would solve main problem. Might be reasonable and will contribute? Hmm.\n\nThe proposal claims compliant. If implemented as described, would solve logging mandate. Effectiveness level 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Could argue 3 = contributes but partial solution because only timestamps and input data mentioned not all requirements. But description says automatically generates logs compliant with Article 12 across all high-risk projects. So assume it solves.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 4? Might be 3 because logging is necessary but not sufficient? Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. It does.\n\nI'll give effectiveness 4.\n\nEvidence: If deployed, mandatory standardized calls would ensure automatic event recording as mandated by Article 12.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text lines.\n\nLet's produce structured output.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1127\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure order.\n\nLet's produce.",
