re is fairly common. Standard middleware logging. Likely common, mundane. Could be 2? It's interesting but not ingenious. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Many labs have logging libraries. Evidence: Automatic event logging libraries are a common industry practice for regulatory traceability.\n\nimplementability (1-4): 4 easy low cost non-radical changes; 3 some changes reasonably feasible; 2 significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible; 1 totally infeasible\n\nImplementing internal software library across diverse architectures, integrate into training and inference pipelines, immutable logs. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3. Not easy low cost? Might be moderate. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Building a standardized logging middleware requires engineering effort across teams but uses existing logging technologies.\n\nacceptability (1-4): 4 common strategies violate no norms; 3 somewhat uncommon but do not offend; 2 offends sensibilities somewhat; 1 radically violates\n\nLogging is acceptable. Level 4.\n\nEvidence: Automated immutable logging aligns with legal requirements and does not violate norms or sensibilities.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem. 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. 3 reasonable and will contribute... partial solution. 2 unreasonable unlikely to solve. 1 solves unrelated problem.\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? Captures system states, timestamps, input-output pairs. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enable recording events relevant for identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, monitoring operation. For high-risk systems point 1(a) Annex III need period of use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons... Proposal captures system states timestamps input-output pairs. Might be partial. Does it guarantee regulatory readiness? Probably contributes but may not cover specific Annex III requirements like reference database, identification of natural persons. So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\nEvidence: The library automates generic event capture which helps traceability but does not by itself ensure capture of the specific Article 12(3) fields for biometric systems.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related. 3 solves implied problem related. 2 may have some benefit within special situation...\n\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal directly targets the Article 12 requirement for automatic event logging for high-risk AI systems.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nLet's produce simple listing.\n\nid P2102\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
