l treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\napplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem directly related...\nDo X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Logging mandate. Yes directly applies. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill it solve? Provides automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, captures timestamps system state changes. Might be partial? Article requires logging capabilities enable recording events relevant... For high-risk AI systems point 1(a) Annex III specific requirements... Framework captures timestamps and system state changes automatically. Might be sufficient? Probably contributes but may need specific fields. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial solution.\n\nLet's choose effectiveness 3? Or 4? It ensures consistent compliance across disparate projects while reducing manual burden. Seems solves logging mandate. Could be 4.\n\nGiven anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Framework seems to solve. Might be 3 because need specific logs per Article 12(2)(a)-(c) and 3(a)-(d). Timestamps and system state changes may not cover all required events. So partial.\n\nI'll go with effectiveness 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... Give <subscale>_evidence first then <subscale>. Likely each line.\n\nWill produce structured.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1052\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
