        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate P1605.\n\nProposal: Automated Research Provenance Framework. Centralized infrastructure to automatically record hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events for every high-risk model. Transforms legal requirement into rigorous scientific tool for auditability. Middleware layer within compute cluster captures telemetry pushes to immutable metadata store. Ensures no experiment run without compliant log. Ensures regulatory compliance while drastically improving reproducibility.\n\nRate subscales:\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nIs it common? Automated provenance framework for AI logging is unusual? Might be interesting. Not expressed before? Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not ingenious surprising. Could be 2 = interesting. Let's think: Centralized infrastructure for logging hyperparameters etc. Is that common? Many labs have experiment tracking (MLflow, Weights & Biases). Transforming legal requirement into scientific tool is a framing. Might be unusual but not ingenious. I'd say 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal weaves logging into science, transforms legal requirement into rigorous scientific tool for auditability, improves reproducibility. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance. That's paradigm stretching? It changes relationship: logging woven into science. Could be 3. Not breaking paradigm because doesn't change what lab researches. It's stretching. So 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nIdea is compliant, immutable metadata store. Acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\nCentralized infrastructure middleware in compute cluster capturing telemetry to immutable store. That's significant engineering effort, not easy low cost. Probably 2? Or 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\". Might be significant change, expensive/difficult. For an R&D lab, building centralized provenance framework is non-trivial but feasible. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Could be 3. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says implement middleware layer... That is significant. Probably 2? Let's pick 2? Or 3? The instrument: 4 easy low cost; 3 some changes reasonably feasible; 2 significant change expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. I think building centralized immutable metadata store is significant change. So 2.\n\nBut could argue it's reasonably feasible. Many labs have similar. Might be 3.\n\nLet's choose 2 to be conservative.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nIt automatically records hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events. Article 12 requires logs of events relevant to risk, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring, and for certain systems period of use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons. Does recording hyperparameters cover that? Runtime events maybe covers. Might be partial. It ensures no experiment run without compliant log. It addresses logging mandate. Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes to solution but only partial? Actually it claims ensures regulatory compliance while improving reproducibility. Could be 4. The problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Automated provenance framework would meet logging mandate for experiments. Might not cover all Article 12 specific items like identification of natural persons involved in verification. But general.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 3 = will contribute but partial solution. Because hyperparameters/dataset versions are not explicitly required by Article 12 (which requires events relevant to risk, post-market monitoring etc). Might be extra.\n\nI'll rate effectiveness 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\nIdea directly addresses logging mandate. Yes. 4.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Could be plain lines.\n\nI'll output JSON with fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1605\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide concise one sentence.\n\nLet's craft evidences:\n\noriginality_evidence: The proposal frames mandatory logging as a scientific provenance system rather than a pure compliance chore, which is unusual for labs.\noriginality: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It weaves logging into the research workflow and presents compliance as an auditability tool, changing the relationship between R&D and compliance obligations.\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Automated immutable logging of experiments complies with EU AI Act norms and raises no legal or social concerns.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Deploying a centralized middleware layer and immutable metadata store across a compute cluster requires significant engineering and cost.\nimplementability: 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The framework will generate automatic logs for model development, thereby helping meet Article 12 traceability requirements while also improving reproducibility.\neffectiveness: 3? Wait evidence says helping meet... Might imply 4. Hmm.\n\nIf effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve problem without regard workability. Does it solve logging mandate? It records hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events; Article 12 requires recording of events relevant for risk, post-market monitoring etc. Maybe not fully. Could be partial.\n\nLet's keep effectiveness 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly targets the lab’s need to meet Article 12 automatic record-keeping and traceability obligations.\napplicability: 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
