d problem of cost containment. Probably applicability 3 = solves an implied problem related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y, which applies). Or 4 = solves identified problem directly related? Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Triage ensures only spend on systems that require them. That's related but not meeting logging mandate itself. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will contribute to solution? It helps manage resources but does not ensure logging compliance for high-risk systems. It's partial. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps, but only partial). Could be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Anchors: 4=paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3=paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners...). 2=slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1=paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nTriage framework is process change but still treats compliance as logging practice? It changes resource allocation before logging. Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1? Standard compliance practice is risk assessment. Hmm. I'd say 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious. 4=not expressed before AND ingenious... 3=unusual interesting shows some imagination; 2=interesting; 1=common mundane boring.\n\nProject triage for AI Act high-risk classification is fairly common, mundane. Could be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Mayb