us...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nBridging logs with provenance is fairly standard in MLOps. Might be interesting but not rare? Versioned data lake linking logs with Git commits is somewhat common. Probably 2 = interesting. Or 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. I'd say it's interesting but not ingenious; many labs do provenance. Could be 2.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 easy low cost or non-radical\n3 some changes or reasonably feasible\n2 significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nImplementing versioned data lake linking logs with Git commits and dataset hashes is significant change, expensive. Probably 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Or 3? Linking logs to research is acceptable. I'd say 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? It ensures traceability to training version, links logs. Article12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of use etc. Linking to Git commits goes beyond but satisfies traceability. Seems will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit...\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nDirectly applies: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes. Score 4.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale_evidence and subscale.\n\nFormat: maybe JSON