lity (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious... Not expressed before AND ingenious? Open standard for high-risk AI record-keeping is unusual interesting; shows some imagination. Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 2? Standardization initiatives are common. Might be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Proposal positions lab as thought leader in AI governance, creates common scientific language. That's changing relationship? Might be paradigm stretching: logging woven into science, new partners/offerings born from compliance. Could be 3. Or slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice -> 2. The proposal is about defining standards, not necessarily changing R&D. It creates standard schemas and API. That is introducing new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. However positioning as thought leader may change relationship. I'd say 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (new partners or offerings born from compliance). Might be 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Defining open standard is acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Could be 3 somewhat uncommon? Probably 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Publishing white paper and reference implementation requires significant effort, scarce resources. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures => 3. Not easy low cost. So 3.\n\nWe need one sentence ev