le. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 = easy to implement at low cost. Might be some changes. I'll give 3? Could be 4 easy. Let's say 3 some changes, reasonably feasible. Evidence: creating a checklist and audit committee requires staffing and process change but is administratively straightforward.\n\nAcceptability: common strategies that violate no norms. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Yes. Evidence: internal compliance audits are socially, legally acceptable standard practice.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). It catches gaps early, but doesn't actually build logging capability. So contributes but not fully solves logging mandate. Score 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of the problem (helps, but only partial). Evidence: audit verifies existing logs meet Article 12 but does not create the technical logging capabilities themselves.\n\nApplicability: solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Audit addresses record-keeping compliance. That's directly related. Score 4. Evidence: ensuring log-traceability before deployment directly addresses meeting Article 12 record-keeping requirements.\n\nReturn format: echo id, then for each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nProbably format like:\n\nid: P1721\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\netc.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure order: originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability, effectiveness, applicability\n\nProvide.\n\n",
