onitoring dashboards are standard industry practice and do not conflict with legal, social or business norms. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds new monitoring tools around existing logs but keeps compliance framed as logging practice rather than redefining R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Combining ELK-based real-time alerting with AI Act risk categories is a familiar DevOps pattern applied to a new regulation. Score 2.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The monitor assumes compliant logs already exist and improves use of those logs, but it does not by itself create the required automatic recording capabilities. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The dashboard directly targets the Article 12 mandate for traceability, risk identification and post-market monitoring of high-risk AI systems. Score 4.\n\nCheck anchors.\n\nCould paradigm_relatedness be 3? \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\" It turns legal requirement into technical advantage by improving debug. That is weaving into science? Might be slightly paradigm stretching =2. Keep 2.\n\nOriginality 2 vs 3. I think it's interesting but common. 2 is interesting. Could be 3 unusual? Hmm.\n\nMaybe implementability 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. ELK stack tailored maybe not low cost. I'd keep 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
